Chapter 6 – Interstellar Percolation and the Great Silence

6.1 The Stellar Network as a Continuum Percolation Problem

The galaxy is modelled as a 3D Poisson point process with GAIA‑calibrated candidate‑star density ρ ≈ 0.017 pc⁻³ (G/K dwarfs). Two independent quantities govern whether colonization can percolate:

(a) Bare geometric threshold. The asymptotic continuum‑percolation relation gives z·p_c → 8η_c ≈ 2.735 in 3D for spherical connection neighborhoods at large coordination number z. At z = p_c·z = 2.735 with p=1 (bond fully present), this is precisely the critical mean degree ⟨k⟩_c:

⟨k⟩_c ≈ 2.74, R_c = (3⟨k⟩_c/4πρ)^(1/3) ≈ 3.38 pc.

For R < R_c, percolation is geometrically impossible regardless of colonization success probability.

