6.3 Resolution of the Great Silence — Formal Statement

The silence is the statistical outcome of most civilizations falling into subcritical percolation clusters. Hanson’s Great Filter is identified as the metropolitan‑dependency phase, quantified by p = P_survive. The percolation parameter is derived from internal thermodynamics and shock history, not assumed. Only quiescent‑epoch civilizations with propulsion speeds above roughly 0.05c and target ranges beyond 5 pc can produce a galactic‑spanning outcome.

Testable predictions (theoretical):

1. Technosignatures, if detected, will be temporally bounded and spatially isolated—transient flares rather than durable empires.
2. A sufficiently deep and complete survey of a galactic region should reveal a power‑law distribution of cluster sizes, consistent with subcritical directed percolation, and no giant component.
3. Discovery of a coherent, long‑lived, galaxy‑spanning empire would falsify the model.

