7.3 Discussion of Patterns

Terminal collapses concentrate where shock exceeded local S_crit and knowledge networks were too sparse to rebuild (Greenland, Indus, Inka). Partial collapses with knowledge ratcheting occur when shocks are spatially heterogeneous (Rome, Late Bronze Age, Maya north/south split). The clearest catalytic clearing—the mid‑20th century—stayed sub‑critical. Exogenous annihilation (Inka) reminds us that the model captures structural tendencies, not deterministic laws. These patterns are consistent with the model’s asymmetric predictions, but no statistical inference is drawn.

