C.5  Link to Chapter 6

The colony success probability p(R,v) derived from the hazard‑rate model (§5.6) must exceed p_{\text{crit}}(R) for colonisation to percolate. The worked example in §6.2 demonstrates that for a turbulent epoch (\lambda_0 = 10^{-2}\,\text{yr}^{-1}), p(R,v) remains below p_{\text{crit}}(R) across all realistic ranges and speeds, while a quiescent epoch (\lambda_0 = 10^{-3}\,\text{yr}^{-1}) can achieve supercriticality for R \gtrsim 5\,\text{pc} with v \gtrsim 0.05c.

Caveat. Near the critical radius R_c, the asymptotic 2.735/z relation is least accurate; the true p_c(R) is expected to be slightly higher than the formula suggests, which would further narrow the supercritical window. This zone, precisely the knife‑edge region of greatest interest, remains the framework’s least precise quantitative element and is flagged as a priority for future refinement via finite‑z corrections or direct numerical simulation on realistic stellar catalogues.


